title: Editing metarecords from the command line
tags: [[Data model]] [[Command line]]
kind: guide
summary: mf metarecord reads, creates, overwrites and deletes metarecords and their fields; mf field works on one row by its id.

The selector comes before the verb ([[Selectors]]): `-i <uuid>` for one metarecord,
`-q '<query>'` for every match, nothing for all of them. Values are field
specifications, `name:type=value` ([[Field specifications]]).

!! Read

```
mf -n music metarecord -i 3f2a… get                       # the full JSON, field ids included
mf -n music metarecord -q 'rating > 3' get                # the matching uuids, one per line
mf -n music metarecord -q 'rating > 3' get --count        # how many — one round-trip
mf -n music metarecord -q 'rating > 3' get --limit 500 --cursor "$token"  # the next batch
mf -n music metarecord -q 'artist = "Nina"' get --select title,rating --tsv
mf -n music metarecord -q 'mfr_type = "dir"' get --resolve-tree mfr_path
mf -n music metarecord -i 3f2a… field get tag --resolve path
```

* `--select a,b` prints the selected fields; with `--tsv`, one tab-separated row per metarecord (each field's first value, empty when absent) — the compact form scripts read. `--values` prints raw values, one per line. `--sort field[:asc|desc]` and `--limit N` shape the list.
* `--limit N` with `--cursor` reads a scope by batches. When the limit stops the read while more match, `mf` prints `next-cursor <token>` on ''stderr'' (stdout stays the data); passing `--cursor <token>` with the same query and `--sort` resumes strictly after the last record printed. The token is the daemon's cursor ([[Pagination and sorting]]): bound to that query and sort (another one is refused), it does not expire, and a record whose sort value changes between two batches moves with it — it may come twice or be skipped. Query selectors only.
* `--count` prints the number of matches alone; it is exact and costs one request whatever the size. Query selectors only.
* `--resolve-tree <field>` prints each selected metarecord's path in that `tree_ref` field, sorted — in one request, the bulk form of `mf path`; with `--tsv`, `uuid<TAB>path`. The repository's root prints an empty line.
* `--eq name[:type]=value` (repeatable, AND-ed) selects by exact value without writing a query: the value is escaped, so quotes in it cannot break anything. Scripts use it instead of quoting `title = "$X"` by hand.
* `field get <name> --resolve <field>` treats each value as a `ref` and prints the referenced metarecords' `<field>` instead — a record's tag paths from its `tag` refs — in one request (a path when `<field>` is a `tree_ref`). Needs `-i`.

!! Write

```
mf -n music metarecord add title:string=Blue rating:int=4       # prints the new uuid
mf -n music metarecord -q 'artist = "Nina"' field add tag:string=jazz
mf -n music metarecord -q 'artist = "Nina"' field remove tag:string=jazz
mf -n music metarecord -i 3f2a… field set tag:string=jazz tag:string=live
mf -n music metarecord -i 3f2a… field unset rating             # the field becomes unknown
mf -n music metarecord -i 3f2a… field set rating:nothing       # "does not apply"
mf -n music metarecord -i 3f2a… set -f title:string=Blue       # the WHOLE record
mf -n music metarecord -q 'rating = 0' delete                  # asks first; --force does not
```

* `field set` replaces every row of the name with the values given (several specs, one name); `add` appends one; `remove` takes out the rows equal to the value, leaving the others; `unset` removes the name entirely — not the same as setting it to `nothing` ([[Nothing versus unknown]]).
* A query selector writes every match in ''one'' revision, one operation per metarecord changed; `field add` prints how many metarecords gained a row.
* `metarecord set` overwrites the whole record — any field not listed goes, `mfr_*` and `mf_*` included, so a careless one untracks a file. It needs `-i` and a mandatory `-f`, so that it is never confused with `field set`. Round-trip the output of `get` to keep everything.
* `delete` of a query asks for confirmation with the number of matches, then deletes them in one atomic request.
* Writing an `mfr_*` field needs `-f`/`--force` ([[Reserved fields]]).

!! One row by its id

```
mf -n music field                      # every field name with its type (field list)
mf -n music field list --type tree_ref
mf -n music field get 42 [--resolve]   # the row, as JSON; --resolve prints a tree_ref's path
mf -n music field set 42 tag:string=jazz   # rename and/or revalue, keeping the id
mf -n music field delete 42
```

There is no `mf field add`: rows are born under a name (`metarecord … field add`).
`field set <id>` is refused when it would make the row the twin of a sibling
([[No duplicate rows]]). To change a field's type everywhere: [[Changing a field's type]].

!! What each command logs

|!Command |!Operations in the log |
|`metarecord add` / `delete` |one `create_metarecord` / `delete_metarecord` |
|`metarecord set` |one `set_metarecord` (all fields, before and after) |
|`field set <name>` |one `set_field` (every row of the name) |
|`field add` |one `append_field` — none when the value was already there |
|`field remove` / `unset` |one `delete_field` (the rows removed) |
|`field set <id>` |a `delete_field` then an `append_field`, the row keeping its id |
|`field delete <id>` |one `delete_field` |
|`retype` |per converted row, a `delete_field` then an `append_field` |

A write to many metarecords is still one revision; `mf log undo` takes it back whole.
